{"step_index":379,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-30T16:42:24Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-30T22:12:24+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-30T22:12:25+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/prompt-forge/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 265\nTotal Bytes: 16181\nShowing lines 100 to 200\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n100: - Has built-in extended thinking — light reasoning guidance helps, heavy CoT is counterproductive\n101: - Be explicit and specific. Front-load everything in one turn.\n102: - Use XML tags (`<context>`, `<instructions>`, `<examples>`) for complex prompts — Claude parses XML natively and it reduces hallucination\n103: - Excels at: complex multi-file code generation, architecture design, debugging, multi-step reasoning\n104: - **Prompt structure**: Place stable instructions first (cacheable prefix), dynamic content last\n105: - **Structured output**: If you need reasoning AND formatting, ask for reasoning first, then formatting — forcing both simultaneously degrades quality (\"Format Tax\")\n106: - Use MUST/ALWAYS only for safety constraints, data isolation, output contracts, and irreversible-action boundaries. For tool routing, style, and ordinary workflow preferences, use calibrated conditional language: \"Use this when...\", \"Prefer this for...\", \"Ask before...\" Aggressive modal language can cause Claude 4.6 to overtrigger tools and skills.\n107: \n108: \n109: **Gemini 3.1 Pro (High)** — Antigravity's long-context powerhouse.\n110: - Superior long-context handling (1M+ tokens) — but curate context, do not dump everything\n111: - \"Lost in the middle\" effect still exists — place critical instructions at the START and END of context\n112: - Add grounding anchors: \"Cite only sources you are certain of. If uncertain, say so explicitly.\"\n113: - Explicit format locks with examples work better than prose descriptions\n114: - Use XML tags for input structuring; JSON sch\n<truncated 4840 bytes>\nask failures**: Vague verb → precise operation. Two tasks in one → split. No success criteria → derive binary pass/fail. Emotional description → extract specific fault. Scope is \"the whole thing\" → decompose.\n180: \n181: **Context failures**: Assumes prior knowledge → prepend memory block. Invites hallucination → add grounding constraint. No mention of prior failures → ask what was tried.\n182: \n183: **Format failures**: No output format → derive and add. Implicit length → add count. No role for complex tasks → add expert identity. Vague aesthetic → translate to specs.\n184: \n185: **Output Constraint Forcing (mandatory):**\n186: When enhancing a prompt, always add one of these output constraints if the original prompt doesn't have one:\n187: - **Format lock:** \"Respond as a numbered list\" / \"Respond as a table\" / \"Respond in JSON\"\n188: - **Length constraint:** \"In 3 bullet points\" / \"In under 100 words\"\n189: - **Deliverable framing:** \"Give me the exact command to type\" / \"Draft the exact email\"\n190: \n191: Generic prompts produce generic outputs. Constraining the output shape forces specificity.\n192: \n193: **Scope failures**: No file boundaries → add scope lock. No stop conditions → add checkpoints. Entire codebase as context → scope to relevant function.\n194: \n195: **Reasoning failures**: Logic task without guidance → add \"Think carefully.\" Over-prescribed CoT on thinking model → simplify to light guidance. Contradicts prior decisions → flag and resolve.\n196: \n197: **Agentic failures**: No starting state → add. No target state → add. Silent agent → add checkpoint output. Unrestricted filesystem → add scope lock. No human review → add stop conditions.\n198: \n199: **Structural failures** (from 2026 context engineering research):\n200: - Negative instructions → convert to positive directives\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
